<div id="2026-11-17-ip-literal-is-not-a-domain" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-11-17 — An IP address is never a site domain</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> <code>Comserv::Model::Site::get_site_domain</code> now rejects every IP literal (IPv4, IPv4:port, <code>[::1]</code>, bare IPv6, and <code>localhost</code> with or without a port) at the top of the sub, before any logging or DB access. Such a request has no site domain: <code>Root::fetch_and_set</code> falls through to the session site or the default site instead of matching a <code>sitedomain</code> row. Two IP aliases were removed: the hardcoded <code>192.168.1.199|172.30.131.126 → workstation.local</code> lookup in <code>get_site_domain</code>, and the same two addresses in <code>_site_domain_for_dev_workstation</code>, which used to fabricate a <code>SiteDomain</code> object whose <code>domain</code> column was the IP string.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> Browsing the app by address silently resolved to the <code>workstation.local</code> row and set the site from a hostname the client never sent — <code>http://172.30.131.126:4003/</code> became site <code>CSC</code> while <code>http://3d.local:4003/</code> is site <code>3d</code>, so the same server served a different site depending on whether the caller used the address or the name, and the 3D module reported "not enabled for this site". It also fed <code>$c->req->uri->host</code> back into subdomain/dev fallbacks as if it were a hostname. Hostname resolution is unchanged: <code>workstation.local</code> still maps through <code>_site_domain_for_dev_workstation</code>, and named hosts (<code>3d.local</code>, <code>3d.zero</code>, real domains) still resolve through <code>sitedomain</code>.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>lib/Comserv/Model/Site.pm</code> (<code>get_site_domain</code>, <code>_site_domain_for_dev_workstation</code>). No schema change, no template change.</p>
</div>
